M	.github/workflows/main_perl-client.yml
M	.github/workflows/packetfence-perl_build_image_package.yml
M	.github/workflows/perl-client_build_package.yml
M	ci/runner-hooks/README.md

commit 3c8cdafe9a07be0586a34f259aef057c49245251
Author: James Rouzier <jrouzier@inverse.ca>
Date:   Wed Aug 19 19:35:55 2026 +0000

    fix(ci): hand the container-job workspace back to the runner user
    
    build_package runs in a container, so as root, and $GITHUB_WORKSPACE is a bind
    mount of the runner's own workspace. 7084238d0b gave the job a checkout step, so
    root now writes .git there -- including the reflog `git checkout -B <branch>`
    creates -- and the build steps, whose working-directory is inside the workspace,
    leave root-owned products behind them.
    
    The next job to land on a packetfence-perl-package-build runner checks out as
    the unprivileged runner user and cannot clean any of it:
    
      File was unable to be removed
      Error: EACCES: permission denied, unlink
        '.../.git/logs/refs/heads/fix/pfconfig-reload-leak'
    
    Restore the ownership at the end of the job, taking the uid:gid from
    $RUNNER_TEMP, which the runner process creates itself, rather than hardcoding a
    uid.
    
    Add ci/runner-hooks as the catch-all for every other container job:
    job-completed.sh, run from ACTIONS_RUNNER_HOOK_JOB_COMPLETED, reclaims the whole
    _work tree after each job, which also covers the root-owned dotfiles that land
    in _temp/_github_home. Installing it is a host-side step on each runner -- the
    runner reads the hook path from its own .env, not from the repository -- so
    README.md carries the install, sudoers and .env bits.

