commit 7084238d0b61d569c95517904eb5a39a56a180dd
Author: James Rouzier <jrouzier@inverse.ca>
Date:   Wed Aug 19 15:20:59 2026 -0400

    Fix/update sereal (#9229)
    
    * fix(deps): upgrade Sereal to 5.010 and package as packetfence-perl 1.2.6
    
    Sereal::Decoder 5.004 through 5.009 never reset the pending frozen-object
    state between decodes: srl_clear_decoder_body_state cleared weakref_av,
    ref_seenhash, ref_stashes and ref_bless_av, but not ref_thawhash or
    thaw_av. ref_thawhash is keyed on the address of each frozen object's
    argument AV, and those AVs are freed as the thaw loop consumes them, so
    entries surviving into the next document are keyed on addresses perl has
    recycled -- a later lookup then matches an unrelated AV and returns a stale
    class stash or an info AV that died documents ago.
    
    pfconfig hit this whenever it repeatedly decoded a namespace holding
    FREEZE'd objects. config::Authentication carries six
    pf::config::crypt::object values, and churning that payload through the
    shared decoder corrupts the decoded structure within ~15 decodes and
    segfaults within ~20, in sv_clear -> sv_unmagic(PERL_MAGIC_backref).
    
    Fixed upstream in 5.010 (Sereal PR #316), which adds srl_clear_thaw_state
    and calls it from both srl_clear_decoder_body_state and, crucially,
    srl_begin_decoding -- srl_clear_decoder returns early when
    buf.start == buf.end, so clearing only on the way out is not enough.
    
    Verified against a locally built 5.010: the same payload that corrupts at
    15 decodes and crashes at 20 on 5.004 completes 50000 decodes with no crash
    and no corruption, and the pfconfig daemon survives 45 consecutive
    `pfcmd pfconfig reload` runs with flat RSS.
    
    Also adds the trailer the 1.2.5 debian changelog entry was missing. Without
    it dpkg reads the start of the 1.2.4 entry where it expects change data or
    a trailer, and the current version has no Date or Maintainer at all. Date
    taken from 9c63e3b74c, the commit that added the entry.
    
    * [perl] Update packetfence-perl
    
    * fix(ci): [perl] build the perl package from the commit under test
    
    The debian12 package job failed validating Sereal 5.004 against an image
    that has 5.010 installed, then looped until install_cpan.py gave up.
    
    Two independent defects:
    
      * build_package had no checkout step. It mounted the runner workspace
        and trusted whatever a previous job left there; it shares the
        packetfence-perl-package-build label with build_image, so it can land
        on a machine holding an older revision. That is where the stale
        dependencies.csv pinning Sereal 5.004 came from.
    
      * That mount landed on /root, and the runner forces HOME=/github/home,
        so the CPAN policy baked into the image was invisible. cpan answered
        its own prompts with the default -- allow_installing_module_downgrades
        [no] no -- and the retry could never succeed. The same lost config
        carries INSTALL_BASE, so a module the job did repair would install
        outside BASE_DIR and be missing from the package.
    
    Check out in the job, drop the volume so /root stays the image's own, and
    set HOME/WORKDIR for the build step. Add git to both images since the
    checkout now runs inside the container.
    
    Also pin the Sereal umbrella distribution: Data::Serializer::Sereal
    depends on it unversioned, and install_cpan.py installs every row in
    parallel, so it raced to pull whatever Encoder/Decoder is newest on CPAN.

