commit 37cdf91a0d82dac2c0cd78b1bbf815c11d1ab251
Author: Durand Fabrice <oeufdure@gmail.com>
Date:   Mon Aug 17 10:49:28 2026 -0400

    fix(authentication): don't skip match-time rule sources on admin login
    
    The admin-rules pre-filter added in 4d68fd9205 skipped any source
    without a configured class=administration rule. SQL and HTTP sources
    build their rules at match time (from the password table row or the
    remote authorization endpoint) and legitimately have no configured
    rules, so DB-stored admins could no longer log in.
    
    Exempt any source whose match method is overridden from the base
    class, since those synthesize their rules dynamically.

M	lib/pf/authentication.pm

commit 6e450329e69e705785272a188b96a11852424e44
Author: Durand Fabrice <oeufdure@gmail.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 12 18:46:07 2026 -0400

    fix(fingerbank): stop re-materializing the config graph on every read_config call
    
    fingerbank::Config::get_config calls read_config unconditionally, and the
    cache-hit path deserialized the whole tied fingerbank::IniFiles object
    graph from the CHI cache and re-tied %Config on every invocation. An
    accounting request reads the config ~40 times (security-event trigger
    model lookups, collector/LocalDB sources, is_api_key_configured), and in
    mod_perl children the previous graphs are never freed - the
    Config::IniFiles section back-references pin them. httpd.aaa retained
    ~81 fingerbank::IniFiles objects (~2.6MB) per accounting Start/Interim,
    growing children to 2.4GB and exhausting the host.
    
    Keep a process-local LOCAL_CACHED_AT timestamp of the generation already
    materialized in %Config: when it still matches the cache and both config
    files are older, return without touching the stored graph. The fast path
    costs one small cache get and two stats instead of a full graph
    deserialization per call.
    
    Validated on a live deployment: 300 forced fingerbank_process requests
    grew a child by ~50MB before, ~100kB after.

M	addons/perl-client/lib/fingerbank/Config.pm

commit ff5ca916f065b8f05c83ed940075ad5bc2ef2c20
Author: Durand Fabrice <oeufdure@gmail.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 12 16:03:19 2026 -0400

    fix(httpd): recycle mod_perl children by connections, not a masked request cap
    
    In Apache 2.4 MaxRequestsPerChild is an alias for MaxConnectionsPerChild:
    it counts connections, not requests. httpd.aaa and httpd.webservices are
    hit by persistent keep-alive clients (pfacct, rlm_rest, Go services), so
    with the default MaxKeepAliveRequests of 100 each connection can carry up
    to 100 requests and the intended 1000-request recycle point was really
    ~100k requests (~30h of traffic). Per-request memory retention in the
    mod_perl children accumulated to 2.4GB RSS per child before recycling
    fired, exhausting host memory.
    
    Use MaxConnectionsPerChild 100 so children recycle around ~10k requests,
    capping per-child growth at a few hundred MB. httpd.portal is unaffected:
    it runs with KeepAlive Off and a PERL_RLIMIT_AS backstop.

