commit 9a84b441dbfa955d8be731f200b12203772ce1bf
Author: Durand Fabrice <oeufdure@gmail.com>
Date:   Mon Aug 10 13:07:54 2026 -0400

    perf(connector): cache dynreverse results for 10s per process
    
    With SNMPUseConnector/radiusDeauthUseConnector enabled (the defaults),
    every wired RADIUS request POSTs to the pfconnector server's
    /dynreverse endpoint from Switch::connectRead / radius deauth, even
    though the server almost always answers 'Reusing existing port'. At
    MAC-auth scale that is one HTTP round-trip per packet and it keeps
    per-switch tunnels permanently alive.
    
    Serve the (host, port) from a per-process cache for 10s instead. The
    TTL matches the server's LAST_TOUCHED_TIMEOUT so a tunnel can't be
    reaped while its port is still served from the cache. pod_direct
    calls (domain join) bypass the cache since their host depends on the
    answering server instance. The cache is keyed by (connector id, to),
    bounded by the number of switches, and reset on fork via CLONE.

M	lib/pf/connector.pm

commit 0f9f2a04517fd86f637c23baefa4adc3587cb212
Author: Durand Fabrice <oeufdure@gmail.com>
Date:   Mon Aug 10 13:06:30 2026 -0400

    fix(pfconnector): stop leaking UDP exit-node conns in chisel tunnel
    
    Two leaks in the exit-side UDP handler (the CoA/SNMP path on a
    pfconnector-remote, the RADIUS path on the pfconnector server):
    
    - udpConns.remove() only deleted the conn from the map without closing
      it, leaving the socket to the GC finalizer. Every CoA, SNMP query or
      RADIUS request from a fresh source port leaked an fd until the next
      GC cycle.
    
    - Over PFCONNECTOR_UDP_MAX_CONNS no reader goroutine was spawned, but
      dial() had already stored the conn in the map. Since the reader is the
      only cleanup path, every conn dialed while over the cap stayed in the
      map forever, permanently referenced and never closed. The stuck
      entries kept the count above the cap, so once crossed, every new
      source leaked and replies were blackholed: unbounded memory/fd growth
      under MAC-auth-scale CoA/SNMP bursts.
    
    remove() now closes the conn, and the over-cap path treats the conn as
    a one-shot write that is removed (and closed) right after the write.
    A per-conn write error now drops that conn instead of tearing down the
    whole SSH channel (and every other conn on it): the conn may simply
    have been closed by its idle reader, and for shared RADIUS channels a
    transient ICMP-unreachable from one backend must not kill the tunnel.

