commit db39dc29ffe07e2ba47453b825d3697615f80bbe
Author: James Rouzier <jrouzier@inverse.ca>
Date:   Thu Aug 6 12:07:54 2026 +0000

    feat(auth_log): record the authentication source type
    
    auth_log only stored the authentication source id, which is an operator
    chosen string from authentication.conf, so nothing in the table said what
    kind of source was used -- AD, LDAP, SQL, Email, SMS, SAML, ...
    
    That left the built-in Authentication reports unable to answer questions
    like "how many failures came from LDAP sources", and a source that is
    later renamed or deleted takes the meaning of its historical rows with it.
    
    Add a source_type column next to source, NOT NULL DEFAULT "" so the
    upgrade backfills existing rows without a data pass. It is a varchar
    rather than an ENUM because there are 34 source types today and the set
    is extensible.
    
    The five pf::auth_log functions that write the column take the type as
    their second argument, next to the source id, and the callers pass
    $source->type. invalidate_previous and change_record_status do not write
    it and keep their signatures. The two paths that record a failure against
    every source they tried pass comma joined lists of both, matching what
    they already did for the ids.
    
    Controller/Authenticate.pm picks the matched source out of the list it
    already has rather than going back to the configuration, and
    Controller/Activate/Email.pm falls back to an empty type when the stored
    source_id no longer resolves, so a source deleted since the activation
    email was sent cannot write NULL into a NOT NULL column.
    
    Null.pm now passes $self->source->id instead of the source id returned by
    pf::authentication::authenticate: only one source is ever passed to that
    call, so it is the same id on success, and on failure the returned id can
    be undef.
    
    Also surface the column in the three auth_log dynamic reports and in the
    Go read API, and update the log_event_auth_log trigger expectation since
    auth_log is an eventLogger namespace.

