commit 494818aa93555e5426fc6cb0c1b78dfdb4223b37
Author: Durand Fabrice <oeufdure@gmail.com>
Date:   Wed Jul 15 15:21:38 2026 -0400

    fix(domains): join AD via on-demand dynreverse instead of static connector tunnel
    
    Cloud domain add through a pfconnector deadlocked: the join dialed
    PFCONNECTOR_SERVICE_HOST:5200, but that static join tunnel and its k8s
    Service port were only provisioned at connector handshake, and create()
    fired the join before committing the domain (discarding it on failure).
    So a first-time connector-backed join could never have the port, hanging
    ~195s then failing with HTTP 599.
    
    Route the join through an on-demand dynreverse tunnel, like every other
    connector caller (Switch/RADIUSSource/LDAPSource):
    
    - handleDynReverse gains an opt-in expose_service flag; on a fresh bind it
      patches the pfconnector k8s Service (ensureDynReverseServicePort) so the
      port is reachable via the ClusterIP. RADIUS/SNMP callers omit the flag,
      so their hot reuse path takes no extra k8s call.
    - removeConnectorDynReverseServicePorts prunes a connector's stale dyn
      ports at handshake before the ActiveDynReverse map is cleared.
    - pf::connector::dynreverse forwards expose_service.
    - Domains.pm create()/update() resolve the join endpoint via new
      connector_join_endpoint() (for_ip -> dynreverse); a missing/disconnected
      connector now returns a clean 422 instead of the long hang.
    - Drop the now-redundant static join-remote block from
      pfconnector_static_connections (NTLM-auth static tunnel unchanged).
    
    This removes both the commit-ordering issue and the reconnect requirement,
    so a first-time connector-backed domain join works immediately.

