commit 4a4cc281ffa5bf06e6fdd8d65798fd87dfd9d744
Author: Antony1711 <231257150+Antony1711@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Tue Jul 7 18:27:24 2026 +0200

    feat(admin): cluster-wide live logs, historical logs, pftest in GUI (#9091)
    
    * feat(admin): cluster-wide live log streaming
    
    In a cluster the live log view tailed only the node the user happened to
    land on. The user had to log into each node individually to triage a
    problem. With this change the view opens one tail session per peer using
    the X-PacketFence-Server header that haproxy_admin's passthrough_admin.lua
    already proxies, then merges the events client-side ordered by timestamp.
    
    The fan-out is gated on cluster/isCluster, so standalone installs (where
    no <ip>-api haproxy backend exists) take the original single-session code
    path without ever sending the cluster header.
    
    * feat(admin): historical log view across cluster nodes
    
    Adds a new "Historical Logs" admin page that lets operators read past log
    content from /usr/local/pf/logs/ (active files plus rotated .log.*.gz)
    across every cluster node. Before this PR rotated logs were only
    reachable via SSH on each node individually, so retroactive triage was
    manual and error-prone.
    
    Backend
    - Logs::History serves OPTIONS (allow-list mirrored from pfconfig
      resource::syslog_files) and POST /api/v1/logs/history. The request and
      response shape is inspired by the SaaS eslogs/tail cursor protocol so
      the frontend code path stays unified; the divergences (full-filename
      cursor keys, null-cursor seek to start) are documented in the
      controller POD.
    - Logs::ClusterHistory fans out across pf::cluster::enabled_servers via
      pf::api::unifiedapiclient with a 30 s per-peer timeout. Standalone
      installs detect !$cluster_enabled and call the local controller
      in-process so the response shape is identical on every topology.
    
    Frontend
    - New $_historical_logs Vuex module with cursor-paginated "Load more".
    - Per-host cursor map so a fast peer does not starve a slow one.
    - Routes registered next to LiveLogs; ACL is 'read system'.
    
    * feat(admin): pftest tool in admin GUI under Auditing
    
    Surfaces the pftest CLI's authentication and profile_filter subcommands
    as cluster-wide checks reachable from Auditing > pftest. Operators no
    longer need to SSH into each node to validate auth-source configuration
    or profile-filter behaviour after a config change.
    
    Backend
    - Pftest spawns bin/pftest via safe_pf_run with accepted_exit_status
      [0..255] so non-zero exits (failed-auth case) still return the captured
      output. ANSI escapes are stripped for the JSON response and the raw
      output is kept under output_raw.
    - Pftest::Cluster fans out to pf::cluster::enabled_servers in parallel
      using Mojo::IOLoop subprocesses and Mojo::Promise->all, with a 30 s
      per-peer timeout to stay safely below haproxy_admin's 90 s server
      timeout even when an LDAP/SAML source hangs.
    - Standalone fallback runs the local controller in-process.
    - Routes are auditable; the base Controller's fields_to_mask masks
      'password' in the admin_api_audit_log automatically — no override
      needed.
    
    Frontend
    - New Auditing/pftest page with tabs for authentication and
      profile_filter; results rendered per host with exit-code badges.
    - Password input lives in component-local ref state (no Vuex, cleared
      on submit) so it cannot leak via persisted state.
    - Auditing parent meta.can extended with 'services' so a SERVICES_READ
      admin can reach the page.
    
    * fix(admin): pftest in-process invocation + assorted polish
    
    Found while validating against the live cluster:
    
    - Pftest.pm shelled out to /usr/local/pf/bin/pftest which is NOT
      present inside the pfperl-api container (only /usr/local/pf/lib is
      bind-mounted; bin lives in the image). The endpoint always returned
      exit_code=-1 with "No such file or directory". Replaced with an
      in-process invocation of pf::pftest::<subcmd> through Capture::Tiny so
      STDOUT/STDERR are captured cleanly. Subcommand allow-list stays
      enforced.
    - Password is now optional for authentication (matches CLI semantics:
      empty pw turns the test into a "is this source reachable" probe).
      Frontend submit button no longer requires it.
    - ClusterHistory was iterating peers serially with a 30 s timeout each;
      on a 3-node cluster the worst case approached the haproxy_admin 90 s
      server timeout. Rewrote with Mojo::IOLoop->subprocess + Promise->all,
      per-peer timeout dropped to 25 s.
    - History defaulted floor_ms to undef when no start/cursor was passed,
      which caused it to scan every .gz rotation back to retention and
      return ancient events first. Now defaults to "last 24h" and clamps
      end_ms to "now + 1 min" so future-dated rsyslog timestamps survive.
    - History dropped non-ISO continuation lines (stack traces, multi-line
      events) silently. They now inherit the previous event's timestamp and
      remain visible.
    - Missing FontAwesome icons (cubes, eraser, clipboard, arrow-down) added
      to main.js — without them the new views logged "Invalid prop name"
      for every render and showed blank icon slots.
    - pftest TheView used @click on b-tab which does not fire for keyboard
      activation, so the subcmd in the store could lag behind the visible
      tab. Switched to @input on b-tabs.
    - HistoricalLogs TheView guarded against undefined events on first
      paint (white screen + Vue render error before submodule registered).
    
    * fix(admin): cluster live logs — stop, source attribution, navbar entry
    
    Three concrete things the user hit:
    
    1. Stopping a peer's session returned "unable to find this session" from
       the Go log-tailer (404). Root cause: LOG_GROUP_START only stored
       per-peer session_ids in _groups, so onStopSession/destroySession had
       to read state.session.peer back from each submodule — which on a
       page reload (or a quick stop-after-create) was undefined, so no
       X-PacketFence-Server header was set and every DELETE landed on the
       local node. Now _groups[group_id] holds full peer objects
       {hostname, management_ip, session_id} and the stop path threads the
       peer explicitly all the way through api.delete.
    
    2. Merged event stream did not show which node / which file each line
       came from. Added a colour-coded "hostname / filename" tag (stable
       hash → 6 buckets) rendered ahead of every event in both raw and
       color modes so a cluster stream is unambiguously attributable.
    
    3. Historical Logs had no navigation entry — only reachable via direct
       URL. Added a "history" icon next to the LiveLogs scroll icon in the
       top navbar.
    
    * fix(admin): cluster race + UX overhaul for pftest and historical logs
    
    LiveLogs cluster fan-out (root-cause of the persisting 404):
      createSession raced cluster/getConfig — when the user clicked Start
      before the cluster module had finished loading state.cluster.servers,
      isCluster was still false, the code took the legacy single-session
      path, and stopping the resulting "phantom" peer sessions 404'd because
      no X-PacketFence-Server header was ever attached. createSession now
      awaits cluster/getConfig if servers is empty before deciding to fan
      out. Also extracted startSessions() as a top-level helper so the
      decision/dispatch logic has a single return path.
    
    pftest UX:
      - Authentication form: replaced the comma-separated source-id textbox
        with a multiselect populated from /api/v1/config/sources so the user
        sees what's actually configured and can pick a subset visually.
      - Profile filter form: replaced the freeform key=value string with
        one row per attribute — attribute name is a typed picker (with
        helpful labels and a placeholder per known attribute), value field
        next to it, +/- buttons. The known-attribute list mirrors
        %PROFILE_FILTER_TYPE_TO_CONDITION_TYPE in
        lib/pf/factory/condition/profile.pm so users see exactly the keys
        that drive connection-profile matching.
      - Results panel: exit_code is now rendered with three distinct
        badges (PASS for 0, RESULT for >0, ERROR for <0) so a failed-auth
        result (exit 1) is presented as informative output rather than
        looking like the API call broke.
    
    HistoricalLogs date/time UX:
      - Replaced two free-text ISO-8601 inputs with browser-native
        <input type="datetime-local"> bound to the user's local timezone
        plus quick-range buttons (Last 1h, 6h, 24h, 7d, Clear). The submit
        handler converts local time to UTC ISO-8601 before sending.
      - Files moved from base-input to multiselect for consistency with the
        pftest source picker; the form now defaults to "Last 1h" on first
        render so a click on Load immediately returns recent events.
    
    * fix(admin): historical logs returned empty + UX gaps + colored pftest
    
    Three things the user actually hits, beyond what unit-testing on the
    host caught:
    
    1. HistoricalLogs returned 0 events. Root cause: /usr/local/pf/logs is
       not bind-mounted into the pfperl-api container, only /lib /conf /html
       and a few others are. The new historical controller reads from that
       path so every cluster_history call resolved to {events: [], ...}
       even though the host-side in-process test happily returned 500. Add
       a read-only mount in sbin/pfperl-api-docker-wrapper. After a
       systemctl restart the same query returns 500 events through HTTP.
    
    2. No way back to change the query. Added an "Edit query" / "New
       session" outline button in the LiveLogs and HistoricalLogs view
       headers that routes back to the form route.
    
    3. pftest output was colour-less because pf::util::console::colors()
       only emits ANSI when is_interactive() (IO::Interactive) is true, and
       Capture::Tiny redirects STDOUT to a pipe so it never is. Rather than
       wrestle with that, render the same palette client-side based on the
       exact phrases pftest emits ("Authentication SUCCEEDED/CHALLENGE/
       FAILED", "Found '<n>' profile for", etc.) on a dark monospace
       background so the output reads like a terminal.
    
    * fix(admin): drop pftest status chip + add MAC validation
    
    - Removed the PASS/RESULT/ERROR badge on each per-host result card.
      Since the terminal-style output below is now coloured per phrase
      (success green, fail red, etc.) the chip was redundant. The card
      header now just shows the hostname and a Copy button.
    - Exit code is no longer displayed in the regular case. The CLI's
      pf::pftest::authentication::_run() does not explicitly return a
      status — its last expression is a `print`, so run() resolves to 1.
      Surfacing that as "exit 1" wrongly suggests the API call failed
      when the per-source SUCCEEDED/FAILED lines in the output already
      carry the real result. We still surface exit_code if it is
      negative (controller-side error) with an explicit "controller
      error" label.
    - Profile-filter MAC field now validates client-side using the same
      twelve-hex-digits rule as pf::util::clean_mac. Submit button stays
      disabled until a valid MAC is entered, normalized "aa:bb:..." form
      is shown as a valid-feedback chip, and a too-short or malformed
      value gets red invalid-feedback text instead of a 422 round-trip.
    
    * fix(admin): pass DELETE header via 3-arg apiCall.delete form
    
    Real root cause of the persistent "Unable to find this session" 404 on
    stopping a cluster-wide LiveLogs session.
    
    src/utils/api.js overrides apiCall.delete with a (url, data, config)
    signature — DELETE is grouped together with post/put/patch under
    `methodsWithData`, not with the no-data verbs. The previous call
    
        apiCall.delete([prefix(), 'tail', id], serverConfig(server))
    
    passed serverConfig(server) — i.e. `{ headers: { 'X-PacketFence-Server':
    ip } }` — as the request BODY instead of the request config. The
    header never reached the wire, so passthrough_admin.lua kept routing
    every DELETE to the default `api` backend (haproxy LB to the local
    node), and the Go log-tailer there had no such session_id -> 404.
    
    All earlier fixes around this bug (cluster/getConfig race, full peer
    objects in _groups, explicit peer pass-through to stopSession) were
    correct but hidden by this signature mismatch. Confirmed in
    haproxy_admin.log: DELETE entries show `the default api backend` as the
    chosen backend, while GET — which uses the (url, config) form via
    methodsWithoutData — shows the correct `<peer-ip>-api`.
    
    Fix is one line: pass null as the data argument so serverConfig lands
    in the third (config) slot.
    
    POST and touch (postQuiet) already used the 3-arg form correctly,
    which is why session creation and keep-alive always worked.
    
    * fix(admin): swallow 404 on LiveLogs stop, bypass apiCall.delete alias
    
    Even with the (url, null, config) form correctly passing
    X-PacketFence-Server, a 404 on DELETE is still possible (golongpoll
    idle-evicted the session, peer restarted, browser is on a stale cached
    bundle, etc). The shared response interceptor in utils/api.js raises a
    red "Unable to find this session" notification on every 404 because the
    Go log-tailer's response has no `quiet: true` field — which is exactly
    what the user keeps seeing.
    
    Two fixes folded into one call:
    - call apiCall.request directly (skip the (url, data, config) alias
      whose signature already cost us the header), set
      validateStatus to accept 404 as a success so the interceptor goes
      through the success path, and transformResponse to inject
      `quiet: true` so the success-path interceptor does not pop a message
      either.
    - cleanup is best-effort by design: the UI just needs the session
      unregistered, not the server-side acknowledgement.
    
    * style(admin): tighten comments to one-line WHYs
    
    * docs(api): OpenAPI for logs/history, logs/cluster_history, and pftest endpoints
    
    Adds the four new endpoints to docs/api/spec/static/:
    - /api/v1/logs/history (OPTIONS, POST)
    - /api/v1/logs/cluster_history (POST)
    - /api/v1/pftest/{authentication,profile_filter} (POST)
    - /api/v1/pftest/cluster/{authentication,profile_filter} (POST)
    
    Plus the supporting requestBodies, responses, and schemas in static/.
    The merged openapi.yaml / openapi.json are intentionally not regenerated
    here so the diff stays reviewable; CI / a maintainer can regenerate via
    docs/api/spec/generate-openapi-spec.pl.
    
    * docs(news): add cluster live logs, historical logs, pftest GUI entries
    
    * refactor(api): pftest runners as package subs, drop fragile ->new(%$self)
    
    Review: _run_authentication/_run_profile_filter are pure functions of the
    request body, so constructing a second controller via ->new(%$self) (which
    shallow-copies tx/stash/match by reference) just to reach them was fragile.
    They are now package subs run_authentication($body)/run_profile_filter($body);
    the route-facing instance methods stay as thin render wrappers and the
    Cluster dispatcher calls the package subs directly.
    
    Also repairs t/unittest/UnifiedApi/Controller/Pftest.t: it mocked a
    nonexistent _spawn sub (the controller invokes in-process via _invoke) and
    asserted 422 for a missing password, contradicting the documented
    optional-password behavior. Tests now mock _invoke, cover the
    empty-password forwarding and the subcommand allow-list.
    
    The same pattern in Logs/ClusterHistory.pm is resolved by a later commit
    that removes the Perl historical-log controllers entirely.
    
    * feat(go): time-window history mode in log-tailer — RE2 filters, byte-offset cursors, bounded scans
    
    Implements the reviewer-preferred architecture for the historical log view:
    instead of a parallel Perl reimplementation of the log parsing, the existing
    caddy2 log-tailer plugin gains a stateless POST/OPTIONS /api/v1/logs/history
    that reads the at-rest files (active + .log.N.gz rotations). The Perl
    controllers are removed in a follow-up commit.
    
    Review findings addressed at the root:
    - ReDoS (blocking): filters are RE2 (regexp.Compile) — immune to
      catastrophic backtracking; invalid patterns are a 422; 256-char cap.
    - Unbounded scan: wall-clock budget (5s) + decompressed-byte ceiling
      (256MB) + event cap; cursors advance over non-matching regions and a
      bounded page returns truncated=true so the client resumes losslessly.
    - Cursor boundary loss: cursors are byte positions (source rotation index,
      uncompressed offset, content signature), not timestamps — pages can no
      longer drop lines that share a millisecond. The signature pins a fixed
      hashed prefix length (append-only files keep it stable), detects
      logrotate renames so the offset survives rotation, never matches empty
      content, and forged client values are clamped to the timestamp fallback.
    - Duplication: ISO-8601 parsing/level mapping lives once in logmeta.go and
      is shared by live tail and history (ExtractMetaISO); the live tailer
      previously produced empty meta for the ISO lines rsyslog actually writes.
    
    The filename allow-list (basenames from the syslog_files resource, resolved
    strictly under /usr/local/pf/logs) is enforced before any path is built —
    the property the reviewer verified as good in the Perl version. Hygiene in
    the existing tail handler: regexp.Compile instead of MustCompile on user
    input (panic -> 422) and a missing return after the empty-files 422. The
    api-frontend logs mount is tightened to read-only (only rsyslogd writes
    there).
    
    Includes regression tests for window/meta extraction, pagination across
    same-millisecond runs, rotation between polls, empty-file signature cycles,
    continuation lines at page boundaries, anomalous future timestamps, and
    traversal attempts.
    
    * feat(admin): historical logs query peers directly via X-PacketFence-Server
    
    With /api/v1/logs/history served by the Go log-tailer in every node's
    api-frontend, the cluster fan-out moves to the client: one request per peer
    through the existing X-PacketFence-Server haproxy routing — the same
    mechanism the cluster LiveLogs sessions use — so the server-side
    /logs/cluster_history fan-out endpoint becomes unnecessary.
    
    This also fixes two review-flagged cursor problems in the store: cursors
    are now opaque per-node byte-position objects kept strictly per host and
    replayed verbatim to their own peer (the old numeric max-merge collapsed
    them across hosts, silently degrading every other peer), and the new
    truncated flag is honored so a budget-truncated empty page no longer ends
    'Load more' prematurely. A failed peer surfaces as an error without
    poisoning the other peers' results and never marks the session exhausted.
    
    * refactor(api): drop Perl historical-log controllers in favor of go log-tailer history mode
    
    Resolves the review's architectural finding: Logs/History.pm reimplemented
    the Go log-tailer's line parsing, level mapping, .gz rotation enumeration
    and filter handling in Perl. That time-window mode now lives in the
    log-tailer plugin itself (one parser for live tail and history), so the
    Perl controllers, their routes, their unit tests and the pfperl-api logs
    bind-mount are removed. The /logs/cluster_history fan-out endpoint
    disappears with them — the frontend queries each node directly via
    X-PacketFence-Server.
    
    The remaining ->new(%$self) instance the reviewer flagged in
    Logs/ClusterHistory.pm:37 goes away with the file.
    
    OpenAPI statics describe the new contract: opaque byte-position cursors,
    the truncated flag, RE2 filter semantics with the 256-char cap, and
    per-node querying via the X-PacketFence-Server header.
    
    * fix(aaa): dedicated PFTEST admin actions; align pftest GUI ACL with API authorization
    
    Review: /api/v1/pftest/* had no entry in pathAdminRolesMap and was only
    protected by the implicit SYSTEM fall-through, while the GUI route declared
    can: 'read services' — a SERVICES_READ-only admin saw the form and got a
    403 on submit.
    
    The protection is now intentional and aligned on both sides, using the
    more specific role the reviewer suggested:
    
    - new PFTEST_READ/PFTEST_CREATE admin actions in @ADMIN_ACTIONS (the
      admin-roles form, the ALL role and the frontend acl derivation pick
      them up automatically)
    - explicit adminRoleMapping /api/v1/pftest/ -> PFTEST, with a test that
      PFTEST_CREATE is authorized on all four endpoints and that SERVICES_READ
      and SYSTEM_CREATE are now denied
    - frontend can: 'create pftest' on the route, the Auditing nav item, and
      the Auditing parent guard (the create verb cannot ride the existing
      $some('read', ...) list)
    
    For admins holding only PFTEST_CREATE the source picker degrades to empty
    (config/sources needs USERS_SOURCES_READ; the call is already
    quiet/catch-guarded).
    
    * fix(admin): opt-in pftest cluster fan-out + per-user rate limit to prevent auth amplification
    
    Review: fanning one pftest authentication call out to every peer meant N
    real LDAP/AD/RADIUS attempts per click — enough to trip account-lockout
    policies and indistinguishable from a distributed brute force.
    
    - Fan-out is now opt-in: the cluster endpoints run on the local node only
      unless the body carries cluster:true (the GUI exposes it as a 'Run on
      all cluster nodes' checkbox, shown only on clusters, with a lockout
      warning).
    - Authentication runs are rate-limited per tested user: one run per node
      per 10s window (pf::CHI namespace 'pftest', redis-backed so all workers
      on a node share it), HTTP 429 beyond that. The cluster entry point
      checks before dispatching; the per-node public endpoint checks again as
      defense in depth. The entry node runs its own share in-process (no HTTP
      hop to itself), so the first fan-out can never rate-limit itself.
      profile_filter performs no authentication and is not limited.
    - The 429/message now surfaces in the pftest view via an alert.
    
    * refactor(admin): extract shared log-session helpers and loadClusterConfig
    
    Review: HistoricalLogs/_store/session.js mirrored LiveLogs/_store/session.js
    (~40% identical) and loadClusterConfig() was byte-identical in both
    _router.js files.
    
    The pieces that actually drift on fixes now live once:
    - utils/logEvents.js: defaultScopes, addMeta/delMeta, the isFiltered/
      eventsFiltered getter bodies and the UPDATE_FILTERS computation. Both
      stores unify on the sorting addMeta variant (sidebar values stay stably
      ordered while streaming).
    - utils/cluster.js (added with the pftest opt-in): both log routers now
      import the cluster-config preload instead of carrying local copies.
    
    Side fix while unifying: LiveLogs LOG_FILTER_ENABLE/DISABLE used plain
    assignment on a key that does not exist before the first toggle — not
    reactive in Vue 2; both stores now use Vue.set like HistoricalLogs already
    did.
    
    * fix(admin): in-results text search for historical logs, prefill query form from session
    
    User-reported: after clicking Load there was no way to text-search the
    results — the form's pre-query filter was gone and the results view never
    rendered a search UI, although the session store already carried the
    searchQuery/searchIsRegex machinery.
    
    - Ports the LiveLogs search bar (find, regex toggle, prev/next with
      wraparound, match counter, highlight + scroll-to-match) to the
      historical results view; content is HTML-escaped before the <mark>
      highlight, same as the LiveLogs implementation the reviewer verified.
    - 'Edit query' now prefills the form from the previous session (files,
      filter, regex flag, time range) instead of starting blank.
    - Surfaces per-peer load errors in the results view (the store collected
      them since the per-peer fan-out change but nothing displayed them).
    - Adds badge styling for the lowercase log levels the Go backend emits.
    
    * fix(admin): annotate already-gone live-log sessions on stop instead of silently swallowing 404
    
    Review (non-blocking): treating a stop 404 as quiet success also hid the
    wrong-session-id / wrong-cluster-node cases. The stop stays idempotent and
    silent for the user — the server reaps idle sessions after 5 minutes, so
    this race is routine — but the result now carries gone:true and a
    console.debug breadcrumb with the session id and peer, so a 404 that
    turns out to matter stays diagnosable. The backend returns a plain 404
    either way, so a stronger distinction is not available client-side.
    
    * fix(admin): one live-logs tab per cluster group instead of per peer
    
    A cluster tail rendered one tab per peer session while the route showed
    the merged group stream, which matched no tab: the tab index resolved to
    -1 and BootstrapVue visually activated the first node's tab, so it
    appeared to display every node's logs until the user switched tabs.
    
    Tabs now map to what the route can actually show: one tab per cluster
    group (the merged view, labelled with the node count) or per standalone
    session. Per-node inspection uses the hostname scope in the filter
    sidebar. Closing a group tab keeps stopping every peer session through
    destroySession. The unused groupPeers getter is removed.
    
    * fix(aaa): quantize token sliding-expiration writes to stop Galera deadlocks
    
    TouchTokenInfo rewrote the token row's expires_at on every authenticated
    request. With cluster admin pages polling several times per second per
    node using the same token, every Galera node updated the same chi_cache
    row concurrently, producing steady certification conflicts logged as
    "Error 1213: Deadlock found" on a multi-master setup.
    
    The expiration is now quantized to bucket boundaries (a tenth of the
    inactivity timeout, clamped to [1s, 60s]) and only written when it
    actually advances: within a bucket every node computes the identical
    target, so all but the first touch match zero rows, produce no writeset
    and cannot conflict. The effective inactivity timeout becomes
    [timeout-granularity, timeout]; /api/v1/token_info now reports the
    bucket-quantized expiry.
    
    A loser of the residual microsecond race re-runs the conditional UPDATE
    once — it serializes behind the winning writeset and matches zero rows,
    because the winner wrote the identical value. Only exhaustion of that
    retry or a non-conflict error is still logged as an error.
    
    * fix(admin): make scope filters work in the merged cluster live-logs view
    
    Clicking a sidebar filter dispatched a per-module toggle to every peer
    submodule, but each module only knows the scope values it has seen
    itself — for the hostname scope, only its own node. Setting the filter
    flag on a missing value threw inside the mutation, aborting the fan-out
    so the remaining peers were never updated: clicks on foreign hostnames
    did nothing (until addMeta happened to create the value later), and a
    partially-set filter never changed the merged output.
    
    The view now computes the target state once from the merged scopes and
    sets it explicitly on every peer (new setFilter action; the mutation
    creates missing values at count 0), so peers cannot throw or toggle in
    opposite directions. Sidebar items also keyed on count/filter, which
    recreated the element on every poll and dropped clicks that spanned an
    update — keys are now the stable value name.
    
    Drive-bys in the same code: addMeta's unknown-scope branch dereferenced
    the missing scope, and HistoricalLogs' Clear left stale filters active
    because it never recomputed them.
    
    * fix(admin): historical-logs visual parity with live logs
    
    The historical view rendered its line prefixes white-on-white: the
    live-logs component's unscoped style block globally paints
    .log-timestamp/.log-hostname/.log-process white inside .background-white,
    expecting the level-tinted chip backgrounds the historical markup never
    applied. The shared stream styles (.log, chips, source tags, scroll,
    search bar) now live in src/styles/_log-events.scss, loaded globally —
    which also removes the historical view's silent dependency on the
    live-logs route chunk having been visited — and the historical view
    renders the same colored source tag and meta chips as the live view
    (hostColorIndex moved to utils/logEvents.js). Levels debug/trace/fatal
    gained chip colors; they were white-on-white in the live view too.
    
    The query form capped the To timestamp with :max bound to a computed
    over new Date(): computeds have no reactive dependency on the clock, so
    the bound froze at mount time and the browser rejected current
    timestamps minutes later ("no later than ..."). The cap is removed — a
    future end is harmless, the backend simply stops at the last line.
    
    The Find bar was capped at 26rem in the historical view; both views now
    share the growing .log-search styling.
    
    * fix(go): merge history pages chronologically across files
    
    The history endpoint scanned the requested files sequentially: once the
    page event cap truncated inside the first file, later files contributed
    nothing — a busy first file filled every page, so a second selected log
    never appeared in the results (or its filename filter) until the first
    file's whole window had been paginated through.
    
    scanFile is refactored into a resumable per-file iterator with one event
    of read-ahead, and the handler now consumes the minimum-timestamp event
    across all files: every page is one chronologically merged window that
    advances a single global time frontier. Cursors keep their exact
    placement semantics — an unconsumed read-ahead event is cursored before
    its first byte and re-read by the next page, a consumed-at-cap event is
    cursored after — and the global scan budgets (bytes, wall clock, page
    cap) are unchanged, as is the request/response contract. Per-source
    signatures are memoized since cursors are now snapshotted per event.
    
    * fix(go): broaden ISO log level vocabulary, structured lvl= wins over prose
    
    The ISO level extraction only matched the exact upper-case words
    DEBUG|INFO|WARN|ERROR|FATAL|TRACE, so WARNING, NOTICE, CRIT(ICAL) and any
    lower-case level word produced no level and the admin UI rendered those
    lines unmarked. The word match is now case-insensitive with the
    syslog/apache vocabulary included, normalized through one upper-case map
    shared with the golang lvl= form — which is now consulted first, so a
    debug line whose message mentions "error" stays debug.
    
    * fix(admin): compact log lines — one colored level chip, no syslog chips
    
    Every line rendered the syslog name twice (the Go tailer fills process
    and syslog_name with the same token) even though the source tag already
    names node and file, and the level color tinted all chips including the
    timestamp, so level-less lines looked randomly unmarked. Lines are now
    source tag + neutral timestamp + one level-colored chip + text, in both
    log views; the duplicate "Syslog Name" filter group is gone (process
    remains, events keep syslog_name in the payload), and the source tag
    uses the same font size as the log text.
    
    * fix(admin): unify live-log line rendering with historical view + level-highlight toggle
    
    Bring the Live-View log lines up to the Historical-View design and add a
    shared log-level highlight switch to both views.
    
    - Source tag shows only the log name, not the full path: live tailing
      reports the full file path while the history endpoint already reports a
      basename. New idempotent basename() helper (logEvents.js) applied at
      every source-tag render site in both views.
    - Raw mode drops the redundant hostname token between the timestamp and
      the process: it already appears in the colored source tag. New
      stripHostnameToken() strips it only when it sits right after the first
      token (fails safe on continuation/legacy lines); the full raw line is
      kept for search, copy and save.
    - Raw mode now renders the same colored level chip as the color/historical
      views, so log levels are colored in the Live-View too (no new style).
    - New "Log-Level highlighting" toggle (options.levelHighlight, default on)
      in both the Live toolbar (works in color and raw) and the Historical
      toolbar; the existing Color/Raw switch stays. When off, only the level
      chip color is neutralized (level word stays readable); the host source
      tag color is intentionally unaffected.
    
    * refactor(admin): extract shared useLogSearch composable + in-results search for both views
    
    Pull the in-results text search out of the two log views into a single
    @/composables/useLogSearch composable used by both LiveLogs and
    HistoricalLogs. The composable escapes untrusted log text before any
    <mark> wrapper (only our own markup reaches v-html), keeps per-keystroke
    work cheap (debounced query, Set-based O(1) match membership), and ties
    the "x / N" counter to the exact rendered text. Store-backed
    query/regex stay in each view (LiveLogs fans the write over peers,
    Historical writes one namespace) and are passed in as writable computeds.
    
    * docs+style: trim verbose comments to one-line WHYs, add pftest admin-role example
    
    Condense the multi-paragraph design/benchmark/refactor-history comment blocks
    in the log-tailer history mode, the shared frontend log helpers, and the pftest
    controllers down to the non-obvious WHY; keep all security/correctness notes
    (escapeHtml-before-highlight, clock-anomaly skip, cursor placement, append-only
    signature stability, SigLen clamp, rate-limit rationale, in-process invocation).
    
    Add a [pftest Operator] example role to conf/adminroles.conf.example using the
    new PFTEST_READ/PFTEST_CREATE admin actions.
    
    * fix(admin): scrub password from pftest output, drop unused PFTEST_READ
    
    Redact the submitted password from the pftest authentication response
    (output and output_raw) so it can never leak through the echoed CLI
    output; guarded on length so an empty password is a no-op. The cluster
    fan-out inherits this since each node redacts its own output.
    
    Drop the unused PFTEST_READ admin-role action: pftest is execute-only
    (every route is POST -> PFTEST_CREATE, no read-only surface), so a READ
    action stayed dead. Removed from the constant list and the
    adminroles.conf.example sample.
    
    * test(pftest): cover password redaction in authentication output
    
    Adds unit coverage for the password scrub added in the previous commit:
    a non-empty tested password is stripped from both `output` and
    `output_raw`, and an empty password is a no-op (the substitution must
    not match everywhere). The scrub itself and the PFTEST_READ removal
    already landed upstream; this fills the missing test.
    
