commit 92ce47eb738803ab7d6eb74703b7fa2bd0893992
Author: Durand <oeufdure@gmail.com>
Date:   Tue Jul 7 09:22:30 2026 -0400

    Record node computername directly from the DHCP server (#9124)
    
    * Record node computername directly from the DHCP server
    
    The node computername was only ever written via the Fingerbank collector
    round-trip: pf::fingerbank::process queries the collector
    (GET /endpoint_data/<mac>) and record_result maps the collector's hostname
    to computername. The collector ingests DHCP asynchronously, so for a
    freshly-seen MAC it has the fingerprint (device_class shows up) but not yet
    the option-12 hostname, which record_result then skips. The machine name
    therefore only appeared after a later re-process (e.g. the second DHCP cycle
    following autoreg).
    
    Fix it with an ownership split by network:
    
    - pfdhcp (the Go DHCP server) is now the authoritative, immediate writer of
      computername for the networks it serves. It already holds the client
      hostname and a DB handle, so MysqlUpdateComputername records it (idempotently)
      on Discover/Request/Inform, from whichever packet carries option 12.
    - The Perl listener/collector path defers to pfdhcp for served networks
      (gated on !pf_is_dhcp) and keeps ownership for networks PF does not serve.
    - processFingerbank now forwards the attributes it already parsed
      (hostname/dhcp_fingerprint/dhcp_vendor) to fingerbank_process instead of
      discarding them, and pf::fingerbank::process overlays these in-hand values
      (and writes the hostname directly) so unmanaged-network records no longer
      depend on collector timing.
    
    Caveat: network.hostname_change_detection (default disabled) lives in the Perl
    detect_computername_change; with pfdhcp writing computername first for served
    networks it will not fire there. Move detection into pfdhcp if that feature is
    enabled.
    
    * Trigger hostname_change security event from pfdhcp
    
    The previous commit made pfdhcp the authoritative writer of node.computername
    for networks it serves, so the Perl detect_computername_change no longer runs
    there -- and with computername updated first, it could not detect a change.
    This restores the network.hostname_change_detection feature for those networks
    by performing the detection in pfdhcp itself.
    
    - pfconfigdriver: add PfConfNetwork to read the pf.conf [network] section
      (hostname_change_detection flag).
    - pfdhcp: add a package-level jsonrpc2 AAA client (NewAAAClientFromConfig),
      mirroring pfacct, used to call the existing :Public trigger_security_event
      API.
    - recordComputername() reads the flag (disabled by default, so the hot path
      stays a single idempotent UPDATE); when enabled it reads the stored
      computername and, if a non-empty value changed, logs the possible-spoofing
      warning and triggers internal::hostname_change (security event 1100013)
      before persisting. This is the same event the Perl path raised.
    
    Non-served networks are unaffected: the Perl detect_computername_change still
    runs there (inside the !pf_is_dhcp block).

