    * Expose pfconnector tunnel port 22226 on 100.64.0.1 for bridge containers
    
    The credcache forward POST from ntlm-auth-api-domain (bridge-networked)
    to 127.0.0.1:22226 hit the container's own loopback and was refused.
    Bind the chisel ENV forward on 100.64.0.1 in addition to 127.0.0.1, and
    point CREDCACHE_FORWARD_URL at the host-alias IP so the bridge container
    can reach the pfconnector-server tunnel — same pattern as CREDCACHE_URL.
    
    * Mirror nt_key forward on cached_login hits too
    
    cached_login() only calls update_cache_entry — and therefore push_async —
    on a cache miss followed by a successful RPC. On a cache hit (the common
    case once the first auth has populated the local connector-cache), no
    update fires, so the forward push to the radius-receiving connector
    never happens. Site B then keeps tunneling its MS-CHAPs to the
    AD-hosting site forever, even though the nt_key is already cached
    locally.
    
    Add a forward-only push_forward_async() and call it from handlers.py
    after every successful cached_login. A request-scoped
    g.credcache_forward_fired guard inside _maybe_push_forward de-duplicates
    against the miss-then-update path that already fired the secondary push,
    so the forward POST happens at most once per auth request regardless of
    which path inside cached_login produced the nt_key.
    
    * Gate radiusAuthorize on per-connector probe status
    
    Site B was forwarding EAP-PEAP up to cloud RADIUS even when Site A
    (the connector that owns the AD) was offline, so cloud RADIUS would
    just time out on a missing ntlm_auth_api_remote. Detect that on the
    client side and fall back to degraded — credcache covers PEAP/TTLS
    for cached users, EAP-TLS doesn't need AD at all.
    
    Server:
    - ConnectorStatusTracker probes each active tunnel every 2s by
      opening a chisel channel and hitting the client API's
      /api/v1/service/ping. Inactive tunnels are recorded as down
      without a probe; the map is rebuilt each tick so disconnected
      connectors don't go stale.
    - /multi-domain-config now also returns domain_connector, built
      from each domain's ad_server IP via connectors.ForIP — the same
      CIDR-walk find_connector() does in Perl.
    - /connector-status exposes the live map. /health adds an
      overall ok|degraded rollup (200 / 503) so the no-auth Caddy
      entry from 8cc5bf2308 finally has a backing handler.
    
    Client:
    - Sibling fast cache (5s default) polls /connector-status, gated
      on local tunnel state like mdCache.
    - lookupRealmKey extracted from multiDomainAuthorize and reused
      by radiusAuthorize. radiusAuthorize stays "remote" only when
      the local tunnel is up AND the realm's connector isn't
      positively observed down; otherwise degraded.
    
    EAP-Type isn't visible at authorize time, but degraded is safe
    across PEAP, TTLS, and TLS for the standard reasons.
    
    * Fetch switches fresh in handleRemoteRadiusNas
    
    The chisel server has no periodic PfConfigStorePool.Refresh loop, so
    GetType[PfSwitches] only triggered a real fetch on the very first call
    and returned a stale cached pointer afterwards. New switches only
    appeared after a pfconnector-server restart. Bypass the pool and call
    FetchDecodeSocket directly so each sync-radius-nas.sh run sees the
    current switch list.
    
    * Set ipaddr in synced dynamic-client files
    
    FreeRADIUS uses 'ipaddr =' (not the client block label) to map a secret
    to the requesting NAS. Without it, the runtime dynamic re-parse failed
    with 'Required attribute FreeRADIUS-Client-Secret is missing' and the
    packet was dropped as coming from an unknown client.
    
    * Fix ipaddr to /32 and use nas_type attribute name matching FreeRADIUS v3
    
