    * Align pfconnector-remote with new connector-cache binary layout
    
    The new packetfence-connector-cache binary ignores -conf (path is
    hardcoded to /usr/local/packetfence-connector-cache/pfcc.conf.yaml) and
    expects its SQLite DB at /usr/local/packetfence-connector-cache/pfcc.db.
    
    - Drop -conf from the s6 run unit so the binary uses its hardcoded
      default (and self-seeds the config on first boot).
    - Point init-connector-cache-db.sh at the new DB dir/filename.
    
    * Pin packetfence-connector-cache to 0.1.2-1
    
    This is the version whose hardcoded paths/config layout the previous
    commit aligned the s6 unit and DB init script with. Pinning prevents
    apt from pulling an older or newer revision that doesn't match.
    
    * Wire connector-cache rate-limit endpoint into pf-remote authorize
    
    Adds a rest-ratelimit rlm_rest instance pointing at the local
    connector-cache /api/v1/ratelimit/ endpoint, called in pf-remote's
    authorize after rest-remote.
    
    The endpoint returns 200/remote, 200/degraded (soft deny) or 403
    (hard deny → rlm_rest userlock → Access-Reject). It is called
    unconditionally so a 403 still rejects when rest-remote said degraded,
    but rest-remote's degraded verdict is stashed in Tmp-String-0 and
    restored if rest-ratelimit's 200/remote response tried to upgrade it
    back to remote.
    
    * add openapi doc for pfconnector cache endpoints
    
    * fix openapi spec for connector cache
    
    * Persist connector-cache DB and pin to 0.1.3-1
    
    Mount the persistence volume at /usr/local/packetfence-connector-cache to
    match where the binary and init-connector-cache-db.sh write pfcc.db; the
    previous /var/lib/packetfence-connector-cache target no longer matched the
    binary layout, so the SQLite DB was not surviving container restarts.
    
    Bump packetfence-connector-cache pin from 0.1.2-1 to 0.1.3-1.
    
    * Use a shared pfconnector RADIUS secret on the central radiusd
    
    All pfconnector-remote instances now sign the proxied RADIUS hop with
    unified_api_system_user.pass instead of local_secret, and the central
    recognizes connector traffic by the PacketFence-ConnectorID VSA. This
    avoids the source-IP cache lockout when a NAT'd NAS shares the
    connector management IP, without needing per-connector secret
    resolution or the FreeRADIUS lifetime=0->delete patch.
    
    - chisel server: expose unified_api_system_user.pass via
      /api/v1/pfconnector/radius-secret (handleRadiusSecret), mirroring
      handleLocalSecret.
    - pfconnector-remote configure-raddb.sh (addon + container): fetch
      RADIUS_SECRET from the new endpoint and substitute %password% with
      it.
    - lib/pf/freeradius.pm: add pfconnector_nas_row() and append a single
      nasname='pfconnector' row (start_ip=end_ip=0) to radius_nas with
      secret=unified_api_system_user.pass.
    - raddb/sites-available/dynamic-clients: when PacketFence-ConnectorID
      is present, set control:PacketFence-NasName := "pfconnector" before
      the MAC/IP fallback lookups. Restore Packet-Src-IP-Address as the
      primary IP fallback (revert the session's swap with NAS-IP-Address).
    
    * Only rate-limit the first packet of a pfconnector auth
    
    The pf-remote authorize section called rest-ratelimit on every
    Access-Request. EAP methods (PEAP/TTLS/TLS) emit one Access-Request per
    TLS round-trip, so a single authentication burned through the
    connector-cache rate_limit_rate (default 6) mid-handshake and got
    403-rejected, stalling the in-flight EAP tunnel. This only affected the
    connector path; direct-to-central has no rest-ratelimit module.
    
    Gate rest-ratelimit on the absence of a State attribute so only the
    first packet of a conversation is counted. EAP continuations carry
    State; single-packet methods (MAB/PAP) have none and are still counted
    once.
    
    * Add pfconnector status indicator to admin UI
    
    Adds a status endpoint to the Connectors API that proxies pfconnector-server
    health data, and a status column with colored dot (green/yellow/red) in the
    connector list table with 10-second polling.
    
