    * Address PR review: connector gateway hosts mapping, dynreverse 500 fall-through, and connector status OpenAPI
    
    - pfconnector/ntlm remote postinst: guard /etc/hosts on the 100.64.0.1
      containers-gateway.internal mapping and strip any stale entry, so the
      connector gateway mapping replaces an old 127.0.0.1 loopback line instead
      of being skipped by a hostname-only grep.
    - chisel server handleRemoteRadiusConf: return after writing 500 errors so a
      misleading 200 success body is no longer appended to the error response.
    - OpenAPI: document GET /config/connectors/status returning { items: [{ id, status }] }.
    
    * Address PR review: clientapi loopback gating, status-probe race, drop unimplemented cache API docs
    
    - clientapi: remove middleware.RealIP so localhostOnly gates on the real TCP
      peer; it was rewriting r.RemoteAddr from X-Forwarded-For, letting a remote
      caller spoof 127.0.0.1 and reach the :8081 service-management/credcache API.
    - clientapi: return 403 instead of falling through to an empty 200 when a
      requested service is not in the allow-list (status + start/stop/restart).
    - connector_status: take mu when recording an inactive tunnel as down, so it
      no longer races with probe goroutines writing the same map (concurrent map
      write panic).
    - openapi: drop the /config/connector/{connector_id}/cache/... paths and the
      orphaned ConnectorCache* schemas; no routes/controller back them yet.
    
    * Mask AD machine-account secret for connectors not next to the AD
    
    - remote-ntlm-auth-api-env: return all use_connector domains to every
      connector so each pfconnector-remote's ntlm-auth-api can start and serve
      cached auth, but only hand the real machine_account_password to the
      connector that owns the domain's ad_server IP; mask it (32-zero NT-hash
      placeholder) for every other connector and for unidentified callers. This
      closes the leak where the loader's no-arg call returned every domain's real
      secret to every connector.
    - pfconnector-remote-load.sh: pass the connector's own CONNECTOR_ID (from
      pfconnector-client.env) so the server can decide real vs masked; warn and
      proceed (masked) if it can't be determined, so the service still starts.
    - multi-domain-config: keep the domain view sanitized to
      ntlm_auth_host/ntlm_auth_port/use_connector (the only fields the Go
      pfconnector-client reads).
    
    * Add unit tests for per-connector machine_account_password masking
    
    Extract the masking decision in handleRemoteNtlmAuthAPIEnv into a pure
    maskDomainSecretsForConnector(domains, connectorId, ownerForIP) helper, with
    the connector->owner lookup injected so it can be tested without a live
    pfconfig socket or connectors container. Handler behavior is unchanged.
    
    ntlm_auth_api_env_test.go covers: owner gets the real secret only for the
    domain whose AD it owns; non-owners and unidentified (empty CONNECTOR_ID)
    callers get every secret masked; use_connector=disabled domains are excluded;
    a hostname ad_server is returned but masked; and the source map is not
    mutated.
    
