    * Fix pfconnector sync scripts and systemdmanager error handling
    
    Add missing http:// scheme to curl URLs in the pfconnector-remote sync
    scripts (sync-radius-nas.sh, sync-radius-certs.sh, load-fingerbank-config.sh);
    without it curl treats localhost as a URL scheme and the request fails.
    
    Make systemdmanager.getActiveServices return an error instead of calling
    log.Fatal, so the helper can no longer exit the whole connector process.
    
    * Escape RADIUS secret in configure-raddb sed; drop dead host-install scripts
    
    Escape sed-replacement metacharacters (\, /, &) in RADIUS_SECRET before
    substituting it into the raddb template, so a secret containing those
    characters no longer corrupts the generated config and breaks FreeRADIUS.
    
    Remove the host-install copies of configure-raddb.sh and sync-radius-certs.sh
    (and their Makefile install lines). The pfconnector-remote runs everything
    inside the container, where the rootfs copies are invoked by the s6 startup
    chain; the host copies were never executed and had drifted from the container
    versions.
    
    * Fix typo in exported method: OpenChisselChannel -> OpenChiselChannel
    
    Rename the public tunnel method (and its docstring) and update both call
    sites so the spelling matches "chisel" everywhere, before the typo gets
    cemented across the codebase.
    
    * Guard against non-hash JSON in connector-status response
    
    If pfconnector-server returns an empty body or non-JSON, $tx->res->json
    yields undef (or a non-hash ref for a JSON array/scalar), and dereferencing
    $data->{connector_status} would warn/error. Check ref($data) eq 'HASH'
    before dereferencing; otherwise log a warning and leave the status map empty
    so connectors are reported as 'unknown'.
    
    * Fix syntax error: stray closing brace after ping handler
    
    client_api.go had a duplicate '}' after the ping() function, causing
    'non-declaration statement outside function body' and breaking the
    pfconnector build.
    
    * Fix ntlm-auth-api/join remote not restarted after package update
    
    The remote ntlm services declared only PartOf=pfconnector-remote-combined,
    which propagates stop/restart but adds no startup ordering. Their
    ExecStartPre curls the pfconnector on localhost:22226 and exit 1's when it
    is not yet ready, leaving the unit failed/inactive. The postinst then only
    restarted on upgrade when is-active was true, so the upgrade silently
    skipped the restart.
    
    - Add Requires= + After=pfconnector-remote-combined to both unit files so
      the connector is started and ordered first on every (re)start.
    - Drop the is-active guard in both postinst scripts: always restart on
      upgrade, tolerant of failure (|| warn) so a not-yet-ready connector can't
      break dpkg under set -e; Restart=on-failure handles recovery.
    
    * Make remote ntlm postinst restart non-blocking to avoid upgrade timeout
    
    The previous fix always restarts the remote ntlm services on upgrade, but
    the restart was blocking: dpkg waited on a first start attempt that is doomed
    during the upgrade window and reported its 90s timeout.
    
    The services are Type=forking and only count as started once the monitor
    writes the PIDFile, which it does only after a domain container answers /ping.
    During upgrade the just-restarted pfconnector-remote-combined container's tunnel
    (:22226 / credcache forward) can take up to 120s to be ready, but combined is
    Type=simple so our After= is satisfied immediately. The domain container can't
    reach DB/credcache through the cold tunnel, never pongs, the monitor gives up at
    ~30s without writing the PIDFile, and systemd hits the start timeout.
    
    Use 'systemctl restart --no-block' in both postinst scripts: the job is still
    enqueued with full Requires=/After= ordering, but dpkg returns immediately and
    Restart=on-failure brings the service up cleanly once the tunnel is ready.
    
    * Harden pfconnector-radius from PR review
    
    Address review findings on feature/pfconnector-radius-x:
    
    - freeradius.pm: guard the new non-cluster NAS branch against an undef
      $management_network so a host without a configured management network
      no longer dies and aborts the whole RADIUS NAS-table reload; prefer vip
      over ip when present.
    - sync-radius-nas.sh: validate JSON up front, drop the pipeline subshell so
      set -e/write failures abort, reject non-IPv4 nasname (path-traversal),
      emit secret quoted+escaped with newline/empty rejection.
    - sync-radius-certs.sh: require non-empty PEM-shaped fields before
      overwriting existing certs; printf instead of echo -e.
    - configure-raddb.sh: escape connector_id and mgmt_ip through sed_escape;
      reject a newline-containing secret.
    - clientapi/main.go: side-car :8081 API logs and degrades instead of
      panic/log.Fatal killing the connector; graceful shutdown uses a fresh
      bounded context.
    - server_handler.go: bound credcache OpenChiselChannel with a timeout; log
      per-switch FetchDecodeSocket errors instead of silently dropping switches.
    - systemdmanager.go: comma-ok D-Bus type assertions via asString helper,
      nil-guard Close(), remove dead getActiveServices debug code.
    
    * Remove dead clientapi handler stubs and unused /status route
    
    The collector/connector/ntlmAuth handlers were defined but never wired to
    any route, and connectorStatus was wired only to GET /api/v1/status which
    returned an empty 200 and has no callers (the server-side status prober
    hits /api/v1/service/ping, not /api/v1/status). Remove all four empty
    handlers and the dead route.
    
    * Make pfconfigdriver.CachedHash concurrency-safe
    
    handleClientHandler calls connectors.Refresh on every inbound connection,
    each in its own goroutine, but CachedHash had no locking. Concurrent
    Refresh calls race on cc.Ids: FetchDecodeSocket zeroes then refills
    cc.Ids.Response.Keys, so a second Refresh can read a torn slice with an
    empty key. That empty id reaches Factory.Instantiate, which sets
    PfconfigHashNS="" and panics on the val:"-" guard, killing the HTTP
    serving goroutine.
    
    Guard Refresh and the read accessors with a mutex; add locked GetStruct
    and SnapshotStructs helpers and route the connector/firewallsso reads
    through them so iteration can't race the map swap. Get/ForIP now return
    nil on a missing key instead of panicking on a nil type-assert, with a
    guard added in OpenConnectionTo. As defense-in-depth, Instantiate
    rejects an empty id instead of panicking.
    
