commit 702e567708a083d76ad2544194284ce1ef4b844f
Author: Durand Fabrice <oeufdure@gmail.com>
Date:   Fri May 15 12:48:47 2026 -0400

    DHCP processor: use can_fork apiClient to run notify in-process
    
    pf::client::getClient defaults to pf::api::jsonrpcclient because the modern
    pfqueue worker (sbin/pfqueue-backend) doesn't call pf::client::setClient
    the way the legacy sbin/pfqueue did. As a result every $self->apiClient->notify(...)
    in processIPTasks / processFingerbank / processor_v4 turned into an HTTPS POST
    to localhost webservices -- up to 5-7 round-trips per DHCP packet.
    
    Pin the processor's apiClient to pf::api::can_fork (lazy require to avoid a
    compile-time circular dep with pf::api). Methods now run in-process, and the
    ones marked :Fork still fork as before. Scoped to the DHCP processing flow;
    no global setClient.

M	lib/pf/dhcp/processor.pm

commit 8603a94d467e271522efa5c20a4cb9fe198dfa4a
Author: Durand Fabrice <oeufdure@gmail.com>
Date:   Fri May 15 12:37:57 2026 -0400

    DHCP trigger_scan: short-circuit when no scan engines configured
    
    processIPTasks unconditionally enqueued a trigger_scan task for every
    IPv4 DHCP packet, even when ConfigScan was empty -- in which case
    pf::api::trigger_scan would just return at line 1002. Mirror that
    early-return on the producer side so we skip the Sereal/Redis round-trip
    for the (typical) case where no scan engine exists. Behavior when scan
    engines are configured is unchanged.

M	lib/pf/dhcp/processor.pm

commit c75302f215218c2f410f85f1efa64dc3b11f327b
Author: Durand Fabrice <oeufdure@gmail.com>
Date:   Fri May 15 12:20:38 2026 -0400

    DHCP fingerbank: skip when signature unchanged
    
    processFingerbank queued a fingerbank_process task on every DHCP packet,
    even when the device's dhcp_fingerprint, dhcp_vendor and computername
    hadn't changed since last time. Fingerbank classification is a pure
    function of those three fields, so re-running on identical input wastes a
    general-queue task per renewal. Cache the signature per MAC (process-local
    CHI Memory, 24h TTL matching [storage fingerbank]) and only enqueue when
    it differs from the last observed value. Filter-engine rules still run on
    every packet so their side effects are preserved.

M	lib/pf/dhcp/processor.pm

commit 46baef13637bd54699d7e87d4d1cc4479f4d7bc6
Author: Durand Fabrice <oeufdure@gmail.com>
Date:   Fri May 15 12:00:37 2026 -0400

    DHCP firewallsso: gate Update on half-lease cache hit
    
    processIPTasks fired a 'firewallsso Update' on every DHCP packet whenever
    any firewall had sso_on_dhcp enabled — so each client's half-lease renewal
    queued a general-queue task that almost always re-sent identical state.
    Add a process-local CHI Memory cache keyed by (mac, ip, lease_length) with
    TTL = lease_length / 2; suppress Update on cache hit. IP-change still does
    Stop + Start + Update and re-arms the cache for the new IP, so behavior on
    session migration is unchanged. With the hashed pfdhcplistener_external
    queue each MAC is pinned to one worker, so per-process caching is enough.

M	lib/pf/dhcp/processor.pm

commit 2ecf9bab7919c35e422cdd4547ef6905448771e6
Author: Durand Fabrice <oeufdure@gmail.com>
Date:   Fri May 15 11:53:51 2026 -0400

    pfqueue: hash pfdhcplistener_external by client MAC (4 workers)
    
    Removes the single-worker bottleneck on relayed DHCP. pfconfig fans the
    queue into 4 per-bucket lists and the producer routes via submit_hashed
    keyed on the client DHCP MAC, so each MAC stays pinned to one worker
    (per-client ordering preserved) while different clients run in parallel.
    Adds notify_hashed/local_notify_hashed to pf::api::queue_cluster with a
    transparent fallback to notify() for non-hashed queues.

