commit 4033a528a7afd64dff94426439a6077e838884d4
Author: Durand <oeufdure@gmail.com>
Date:   Tue May 12 15:50:28 2026 -0400

    Fix/pfdhcp duplicated (#9043)
    
    * Fix duplicate IP allocation in pfdhcp
    
    initiaLease ignored ReserveIPIndex errors and still wrote to hwcache, so
    two ip4log rows for the same IP would leave two MACs pointing at the
    same pool slot (visible as error=1 in /api/v1/dhcp/stats). Check the
    error and skip the cache writes; the losing MAC re-DISCOVERs and is
    given a fresh free IP from the pool.
    
    Also harden the other call sites that silently dropped ReserveIPIndex
    errors:
    - handleRequest: NAK instead of ACK when pool/cache have diverged.
    - handleDiscover ping/arp conflict: free then re-reserve as FakeMac so
      the 10-minute cool-down actually applies; OnEvicted now verifies that
      pool.mac[index] still matches the evicted MAC before freeing, so the
      FakeMac reservation isn't undone by the async eviction.
    - handleDecline, handleRelease, ExcludeIP, AssignIP: log a warning on
      reserve failure (behavior unchanged).
    
    * Clarify pfdhcp pool-full DISCOVER log message
    
    The "Nak No space left in the pool" log line was misleading: per RFC 2131
    a DHCP server must not respond to a DHCPDISCOVER when no address is
    available (NAK is only valid in response to DHCPREQUEST), and the code
    already returns an empty Answer rather than sending a NAK. Update the
    message to reflect what actually happens.
    
    * Fix OldestReleased algorithm picking reserved indexes
    
    GetFreeIPIndex iterated DHCPPool.released directly, but that map holds
    an entry for every index (initialized at pool creation, only refreshed
    on FreeIPIndex — never cleared on reserve). With the OldestReleased
    algorithm this could return an index that is currently reserved by
    another MAC, and the subsequent dp.DHCPPool.mac[available] = mac would
    silently overwrite that MAC's binding.
    
    Filter by `free` while finding the oldest, in a single O(n) pass instead
    of sorting all entries. Add a regression test that reserves every slot
    to mac A, frees one, and verifies GetFreeIPIndex (OldestReleased) hands
    out exactly that index to mac B without disturbing mac A's other
    bindings.
    
    * Quiet pfdhcp transaction-lock log and drop redundant global mutex
    
    The "Ignored DHCP request: ... (transaction lock)" line at INFO is just
    a deduplicated relay/client retransmission — the first packet for the
    same (MAC, msgType) is always processed normally. Demote the log to
    Debug so retransmission storms don't crowd out useful info.
    
    While here, replace the manual GlobalTransactionLock.Lock + cache.Get +
    cache.Set sequence with cache.Add, which is atomic under the cache's
    own mutex. The external timedlock.RWLock served no purpose for this
    dedup and added a panic-on-timeout failure mode under load. Same
    treatment for the RequestGlobalTransactionCache check in handleRequest
    and for the GlobalTransactionLock wrapper around hwcache.Items() in the
    stats API (go-cache.Items returns a snapshot under its own lock). The
    GlobalTransactionLock global is removed.
    
    Dedup semantics are unchanged: per (MAC, msgType) and per (MAC,
    msgType, xid) for 1s each.
    
    * Tunable SO_RCVBUF and larger worker pool in pfdhcp
    
    Under burst (e.g. all clients reconnecting after an outage), pfdhcp's UDP
    sockets sat at the kernel default of ~208 KiB, which overflows quickly
    once the 100-worker pool is parked on ICMP pings or MySQL writes.
    netstat -su showed receive-buffer drops correlating with these bursts.
    
    - serverif.go: enlarge SO_RCVBUF on both the broadcast and unicast DHCP
      sockets at open time. Default 8 MiB, override via PFDHCP_RCVBUF (set
      to 0 to keep the kernel default). Try SO_RCVBUFFORCE first so a
      process with CAP_NET_ADMIN can exceed net.core.rmem_max; fall back to
      SO_RCVBUF, never fatal. Log when the kernel clamps the requested size.
    - main.go: bump worker pool default from 100→300 and queue size from
      100→1000 so userspace can actually drain a larger kernel buffer.
      Override via PFDHCP_WORKERS and PFDHCP_QUEUE. Log the effective sizes
      on startup.
    
    To raise the buffer above the kernel cap, also set
      sysctl -w net.core.rmem_max=8388608

