    - Add grub-installer/with_other_os boolean true (allows installation with other OS)
    - Keep bootdev unset so user is prompted with a list of devices
    - Add temporary doc files to .gitignore
    
    This should now show a proper device selection menu and prevent GRUB
    from being installed to the USB stick instead of the target disk.
    
    * usb-bootable-iso: Make partitioning confirmation interactive
    
    The installer was auto-confirming partitioning changes without prompting
    the user to review them. This was caused by partman/confirm being set to
    'true' which means 'auto-confirm without asking'.
    
    Changes:
    - Comment out all partman confirmation presets
    - Let the installer prompt user to review and confirm partitioning
    - User will now see the partition layout and must explicitly confirm
    
    This gives the user a chance to verify the partitioning scheme before
    data is written to disk.
    
    * usb-bootable-iso: Fix GRUB to show device selection menu
    
    The installer has two GRUB device templates:
    - grub-installer/bootdev (string) - manual text input
    - grub-installer/choose_bootdev (select) - device selection list
    
    When bootdev is preset, the installer skips the choose_bootdev menu.
    By NOT presetting bootdev, the installer falls back to showing the
    choose_bootdev SELECT menu with all available devices listed.
    
    Changes:
    - Comment out grub-installer/bootdev to trigger choose_bootdev
    - Set only_debian to true for proper detection
    - User will now see a device selection list (like in netinstall)
    
    * usb-bootable-iso: Fix GRUB device selection to show disk list menu
    
    Changes:
    - Set priority=medium in boot menus to show more installer questions
    - Comment out grub-installer/only_debian and with_other_os presets
    - This forces the installer to ask 'Install GRUB to MBR?' interactively
    - When user answers 'No', the installer shows choose_bootdev SELECT menu
    - The SELECT menu lists all available disks for proper GRUB installation
    
    IMPORTANT: User must select the DISK (e.g., /dev/sda) not a partition
    (e.g., /dev/sda1). Installing GRUB to a partition results in unbootable system.
    
