    - Simplify conditional logic (re-tagging, registry aliases)
    - Use parameter expansion instead of sed where possible
    - Compact image lists and package lists
    
    Net reduction: ~180 lines removed.
    
    * usb-bootable-iso: Force network configuration during install
    
    PacketFence requires at least one configured network interface.
    When DHCP fails, auto-select manual configuration instead of
    allowing the user to skip network setup entirely.
    
    * usb-bootable-iso: Add README with requirements, usage, and QEMU testing docs
    
    * usb-bootable-iso: Call upload target in CI to build and push to Linode
    
    The build_pf_img_usb_iso job was running 'make iso', which after the
    target split only builds the ISO without uploading. Switch to 'make
    upload', which runs build-and-upload.sh — the same build wrapped with
    version handling and the rclone push to Linode.
    
    * usb-bootable-iso: Split build into staged step scripts
    
    * usb-bootable-iso: Fall back to git branch for local image tag
    
    When no CI env vars are set and conf/build_id is absent, derive
    TAG_OR_BRANCH_NAME from the current git branch (slugified like
    CI_COMMIT_REF_SLUG) instead of defaulting to 'devel'. Local builds
    were producing images tagged 'devel' while the installed PF deb
    expected the branch tag, breaking the offline install re-tag step.
    
    The git fallback is gated on CI being unset so it can never affect
    CI builds, which always set CI_COMMIT_REF_SLUG (or CI_COMMIT_TAG)
    and resolve on those earlier branches.
    
    * usb-bootable-iso: Fail build when required packages are missing
    
    The key-package verification only printed [MISSING] but exited 0,
    allowing CI to publish an ISO that would fail offline install
    (e.g., packetfence or docker-ce absent). Track missing packages
    and exit non-zero, with ALLOW_MISSING_PKGS as an escape hatch
    for genuinely DVD-shipped or not-yet-published items.
    
    * usb-bootable-iso: Retry apt-get update on transient failures
    
    A single network blip during chroot package indexing aborted the
    whole build. Wrap apt-get update in a 5-attempt retry with
    exponential backoff so transient DNS/mirror flakes don't kill CI.
    The downstream key-package verification still hard-fails if any
    required .deb is actually absent.
    
    * usb-bootable-iso: Drop bogus grub-installer/password-crypted preseed
    
    "d-i grub-installer/password-crypted string *" is the unset-value
    form emitted by debconf-get-selections, not a real preseed
    directive — the field expects a password type with a crypted hash.
    Leaving it in is confusing noise and risks setting an unintended
    GRUB password on installers that interpret it literally.
    
    * usb-bootable-iso: Stop polluting md5sum.txt with stderr
    
    "md5sum > md5sum.txt 2>&1 || true" let any find/md5sum diagnostic
    get written into the checksum file (corrupting Debian installer
    media verification) and silently swallowed real failures. Drop
    both 2>&1 and || true so errors stay on stderr and a failed
    checksum pass aborts the build under errexit.
    
    * usb-bootable-iso: Install dpkg-dev explicitly in Phase A
    
    Phase B uses dpkg-scanpackages to build a local APT repo from
    cached .debs. Today dpkg-dev is pulled in transitively because
    the preseed's pkgsel/include lists build-essential, but that's a
    brittle implicit dependency — trimming build-essential later
    would silently break first-boot install on offline systems. Add
    dpkg-dev to Phase A's explicit install list.
    
    * usb-bootable-iso: Disable remote PF repo during offline first-boot
    
    Phase A writes /etc/apt/sources.list.d/packetfence.list pointing
    at https://inverse.ca/... so the system can update once a network
    is available. Phase B's apt-get update for the local file:// repo
    runs under errexit and would fail offline because it tries to
    reach inverse.ca too. Move packetfence.list aside before the
    update and restore it after the local install completes.
    
    * usb-bootable-iso: Bundle dpkg-dev in the offline repo
    
    Phase A's explicit dpkg-dev install relied on either the Debian
    DVD apt source or build-essential's transitive dependency to
    provide the package. Add it to the create-local-repo PACKAGES
    list so the offline pf-repo carries it directly — first-boot's
    dpkg-scanpackages step then has a guaranteed offline source even
    if the DVD chain breaks.
    
    * usb-bootable-iso: Rename %%PF_VERSION%% placeholder to match its value
    
    The placeholder %%PF_VERSION%% was being replaced with the value
    of PF_RELEASE_VERSION (the major.minor like "15.1", not the full
    "v15.1.0"), and postinst-offline.sh's Phase A then stored that
    value in a variable also named PF_VERSION — making it easy to
    mistakenly pass the full version. Rename the placeholder to
    %%PF_RELEASE_VERSION%% and the receiving variable accordingly.
    The Phase B variable inside the first-boot heredoc is a separate
    runtime value (read from conf/pf-release) and is left unchanged.
    
    * ci: Point ISO env URLs at Linode object storage
    
    ISO artifacts haven't been pushed to SourceForge for some time —
    the build_pf_img_iso and build_pf_img_usb_iso jobs already upload
    exclusively to the Linode S3 bucket via rclone. Drop the unused
    SF_ISO_REPO_URL variable and rebuild the GitLab environment.url
    links from RCLONE_LINODE_URL (already a project CI variable
    pointing at the Linode S3 endpoint) plus the actual bucket path,
    so "View deployment" lands on a real object instead of a dead
    SourceForge page.
    
    * usb-bootable-iso: Drop SF_ prefix from upload script result dir
    
    The SourceForge mirror is gone, so the SF_RESULT_DIR variable
    (and its results/sf/ on-disk path) are misleading vestiges.
    Rename to RESULT_DIR with a results/ path to reflect the
    script's actual behavior — build locally, upload to Linode.

